#### Carbon trader support
The types of CFM activities supported by the carbon trader are shown in @fig-et_trader_support. As can be seen support focused on aspects of forest management or agricultural extension. Other potential elements of support, such as NTFPs, infrastructure development or market access, featured substantially less.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Types of CFM activities supported by the carbon trader.](CFMG-ET_files/figure-html/fig-et_trader_support-1.png){#fig-et_trader_support width=768}
:::
:::


#### Other income earning opportunities
FGD groups also reported whether CFM had generated income earning opportunities through specific interventions, including market linkages, alternative livelihood interventions, conservation revenue, forest product processing and ecotourism (@fig-et_income_opportunities). Although some community FGDs indicated improvement in income earning opportunities under some categories, especially conservation revenue and forest product processing, most indicated that CFM had not generated new income earning opportunities in these areas.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Income earning opportunities generated by CFM, as reported by female, male, and youth FGDs. The length of the bars represents the number of FGDs reporting a benefit (Yes) or no benefit (No) for each income source.](CFMG-ET_files/figure-html/fig-et_income_opportunities-1.png){#fig-et_income_opportunities width=672}
:::
:::


However, community female, male, and youth FGD groups did identified other additional sources of income generated by CFM (@fig-et_income_sources). Natural resource based value chains featured prominently among these, but interestingly community business was also mentioned.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Additional sources of income from CFM reported by female, male, and youth FGDs. Word size is proportional to the number of FGDs reporting each income source.](CFMG-ET_files/figure-html/fig-et_income_sources-1.png){#fig-et_income_sources width=960}
:::
:::


To better understand the importance of different income earning opportunities we asked the community female, male, and youth FGD groups to rank their top three additional sources of income from CFM (@fig-et_income_ranks). Interestingly, Among the 14 value chains identified, 12 were mentioned as 1st rank at least once. This immediately points to the diversity of forest products being harvested from CFs across the country, but also to the fact that for most CFs only one product features strongly as an addition source of income. Among these, mushrooms and honey are clearly the most frequently mentioned. 


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Ranking of additional income sources from CFM by female, male, and youth FGDs. Bars show the number of FGDs assigning each rank. Only income sources receiving at least one top-three ranking are shown.](CFMG-ET_files/figure-html/fig-et_income_ranks-1.png){#fig-et_income_ranks width=864}
:::
:::


CFMG executive FGDs were asked the estimated annual income generated through CFM (@fig-et_annual_income). Unfortunately, there was a lot of inconsistency in the responses to this question. Most FGD groups did not include income from carbon credit sales, but some did. The figure below includes only those that did not include the carbon credit income to facilitate comparison. 


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Mean estimated annual income from CFM by province, as reported by executive FGDs.](CFMG-ET_files/figure-html/fig-et_annual_income-1.png){#fig-et_annual_income width=864}
:::
:::


Female, male, and youth FGDs also reported which forest products were harvested for household use (@fig-et_household_products). Mushrooms again featured prominently, but other natural resources are also clearly important, such as wild fruits, medicinal plants, thatching grass and caterpillars.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Forest products harvested for household use reported by female, male, and youth FGDs. Word size is proportional to the number of FGDs reporting each product.](CFMG-ET_files/figure-html/fig-et_household_products-1.png){#fig-et_household_products width=960}
:::
:::


CFMG executive FGDs were asked whether the CFMG was collaborating with any private enterprises (@fig-et_collaboration). The answers indicate that only a small number of CFMGs were collaborating with a private enterprise.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![CFMG collaboration with private enterprise as reported by executive FGDs by province.](CFMG-ET_files/figure-html/fig-et_collaboration-1.png){#fig-et_collaboration width=768}
:::
:::


The value chains that CFMGs reported working with through private enterprise partnerships are shown in @fig-et_value_chains. Sixteen out of 17 involved partnerships with honey off-takers and just one involved mushrooms.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Value chains that CFMGs collaborate with through private enterprise partnerships, as reported by executive FGDs.](CFMG-ET_files/figure-html/fig-et_value_chains-1.png){#fig-et_value_chains width=768}
:::
:::


We asked the community female, male, and youth FGD groups to identify economic opportunities that remained unrealised within their CFs (@fig-et_unrealised). Interestingly, although a wide range of different natural resource related value chains were mentioned, timber was mention the most often. This is noteworthy as none of the CFMGs we surveyed are involved in timber production.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Unrealised economic opportunities identified by FGD groups.](CFMG-ET_files/figure-html/fig-et_unrealised-1.png){#fig-et_unrealised width=768}
:::
:::


The ranking of these unrealised opportunities is shown in @fig-et_unrealised_ranks. Again a wide range of resources are mentioned, but the top three are timber, honey and mushrooms. Again, it is interesting that community groups consider their timber resources to have high unrealised potential for commercial development.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Ranking of unrealised economic opportunities by FGD groups. Bars show the frequency of rank 1 (highest priority), rank 2, and rank 3 responses.](CFMG-ET_files/figure-html/fig-et_unrealised_ranks-1.png){#fig-et_unrealised_ranks width=864}
:::
:::


FGD groups also identified potential value-added opportunities from CFM resources (@fig-et_value_added). The overlap with the previous two questions is substantial, but it is interesting that the communities specifically identify honey processing and drying mushrooms, as well as timber processing. This suggests that the communities are aware that there are value addition opportunities that they are currently not realising.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Potential value-added opportunities identified by FGD groups.](CFMG-ET_files/figure-html/fig-et_value_added-1.png){#fig-et_value_added width=768}
:::
:::


We asked the CFMG Executive FGDs to report which primary markets CFM products are sold into (@fig-et_markets). Evidently, most products are sold only at the local market level, with some also being transported to national markets.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Primary markets for CFM products, as reported by executive FGDs.](CFMG-ET_files/figure-html/fig-et_markets-1.png){#fig-et_markets width=672}
:::
:::


### Opportunity costs
#### Precluded activities

The community female, male, and youth FGD groups identified economic activities that are precluded under CFM regulations (@fig-et_precluded). While many of these are self evident, the fact that NTFP harvesting is listed quite prominently is surprising. Whether this reflects a misunderstanding on the part of the communities or indeed that they are being told they cannot collect NTFPs is not clear.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Economic activities precluded by CFM, as reported by FGD groups.](CFMG-ET_files/figure-html/fig-et_precluded-1.png){#fig-et_precluded width=768}
:::
:::



#### Other opportunity costs
FGD groups were asked whether CFM entails opportunity costs for the community (@fig-et_opp_costs). Interestingly, many of the FGDs did not recognise any opportunity costs. However, about a third of the FGDs did. The proportion of FGDs recognising opportunity costs was highest among community male FGDs and lowest among community female FGDs. However, none of the pairwise comparisons were significant.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Whether CFM entails opportunity costs for the community, by FGD group.](CFMG-ET_files/figure-html/fig-et_opp_costs-1.png){#fig-et_opp_costs width=768}
:::
:::





::: {#tbl-M9_et_fgd_opp_costs .cell tbl-cap='Results of the model examining the effect of FGD type and governance index on whether communities report opportunity costs of CFM.'}
::: {.cell-output-display}

